\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The degree scaling and its coefficient pullback}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let a finite cell model #{Y} have #{c_p} labeled cells
in degree #{p}, and let #{F} be free of rank #{r} over #{k}.
Put #{V=\bigoplus_p F^{c_p}} and
#{\chi(Y)=\sum_p(-1)^pc_p}. An endomorphism #{K_0} is
\newvocab{odd in cell degree} when it exchanges the even
and odd summands of #{V}. Both #{\extd+\extdag} and
#{\extd -\extdag} have this property whenever their
incidence operators are defined.}

\p{For a unit #{u\in k^\times}, the
\newvocab{paired degree scaling} #{\alpha_u} on
#{\mathcal A(V)=\Lambda_k(V^*\oplus V)} multiplies both
the barred and unbarred generator associated with a
degree-#{p} cell by #{u^{(-1)^p}}. Its
\newvocab{inverse-Jacobian coefficient pullback} is the
linear functional
##{\mathcal I_V^{(u)}=\mathcal I_V\circ\alpha_u^{-1}.}
The coefficient convention is the
\vocabk{ring paired coefficient}{fdip-00TJ}.}
